  explaining just as if I were just a  beggar at the door and they just closed  the door in my face and finally I went  to their home these friends of my  parents were living on the forged  Passport and they did the same thing and  then I knew I think that was the last -  after there was nothing for me to do for  two days we were scrounging we had no  money we were scrounging around trying  to find food and finally I said to my  sister and we can't live that way but I  died on the street so we might as well  just go to the police  and I went to the police and I said my  name and I said my  name and I said I'd like to join them  and the police were used the first  laughed and then they realized that I  was perfectly serious  and they suddenly I realized that I had  presented them with a problem they  didn't know what to do with me they  didn't know what to do so they told me  that I could sit on the bench and I  spent the night in the police station on  the bench while they were trying to find  out what to do with me  that didn't explain anything to me one  of the policemen  I remember said well they are trying to  find where your parents are and they  finally must have found out somehow  because it's immediately put on a clock  and driven into Hungary and that was in  Sarab which was a gathering point for  the last remaining transports coming out  of practice number because nothing was  going directly from Bratislava I arrived  there to discover that the play two  weeks before my parents had been put on  the train to Auschwitz this was the  first time I heard about Auschwitz I  didn't know anything about it and I was  finally put on one train and it was a  cattle car and I in some ways was  terribly relieved to be there because  here I was among Jews again they were  all strangers but still the fact that  they were Jews was very important to me  I was at that time I was nine years old  and I had my sister who was suddenly a  half and to whom I had to explain  everyt